        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Curved Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/curved-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How does a curved treadmill work without a motor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The slat belt is mounted in a concave arc on a bearing system. Stepping toward the front of the curve lets gravity and the backward force of your stride drive the belt; drifting toward the rear puts you on the rising part of the arc, which slows it. Your position on the curve is the throttle. There is no top speed and no spin-up delay, because nothing is powering the surface except you.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a curved treadmill harder than a normal treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, noticeably. Published comparisons put curved running somewhere around a quarter to a third more demanding at the same speed, measured by oxygen cost and heart rate. Three things cause it: you are doing the work the motor used to do, the running portion of the arc is inclined, and you cannot coast because any drop in effort immediately slows the belt.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do curved treadmill paces match outdoor paces?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, and treating them as equivalent will distort your training log. An 8:00 mile on a curved treadmill represents considerably more work than an 8:00 mile outdoors or on a motorised belt. Either keep curved sessions in their own category or train by heart rate and perceived effort instead, which is the more reliable currency on this machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a curved treadmill good for walking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Not really. The effort penalty that makes it excellent for sprinting makes an ordinary walk feel like work, and the form feedback that benefits runners is irrelevant at walking speed. If walking is most of your intended use, a purpose-built walking treadmill costs a fraction as much and does the job far better.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do curved treadmills need lubrication?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Like other slat-belt machines, the surface rolls on bearings rather than sliding across a deck, so there is no friction interface to lubricate and no deck coating to wear smooth. Slats and bearings are the wear items instead, and both are replaceable — these machines are designed to be rebuilt rather than discarded.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a curved treadmill worth it for home use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is worth it if sprint and interval training is a genuine, recurring part of what you do, and if it is a second machine or you run your steady mileage outdoors. It is poor value as an only treadmill for general fitness, for walkers, for beginners and for anyone wanting a softer surface. Buying used from a gym refit changes the arithmetic considerably.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-09-18",
      dateModified: "2026-09-18",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
