        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A curved treadmill is a <strong class="text-white">specialist sprint and interval
            machine</strong> that happens to be shaped like a general-purpose one. No top speed, no
            lag, no lubrication, immediate form feedback — and a real effort penalty that makes
            steady running and walking worse rather than better.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy one as a second machine, or if sprints are genuinely most of what you do, and
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">buy it used</a>
            — there is no motor, board or deck coating to wear out, so gym cast-offs are unusually
            good value. If it would be your only treadmill,
            <a href="/treadmill-buyers-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">buy a motor</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
